### A complex target can be assembled from simpler functions

@(0.08,0.23) {size=22px, color=#9FB0C5} SIMPLER FUNCTIONS
@(0.08,0.32) {size=35px, color=#51C8F4} NOT · NAND · AND · OR
@(0.08,0.43) {size=35px, color=#51C8F4} NOR · XOR · AND-NOT · OR-NOT

{color=#38516C, thick=6} !arrow (0.47,0.49) (0.63,0.49)

@(0.74,0.33) {size=23px, color=#9FB0C5, anchor=center} COMPLEX TARGET
@(0.74,0.46) {size=92px, color=#FFB36A, anchor=center} EQU
@(0.74,0.62) {size=23px, color=#F4F1E8, anchor=center} nearly 20 interacting instructions

@(0.50,0.81) {size=24px, color=#55E6C1, anchor=center} Selection can retain pieces before the final trait exists.

% SPEAKER: Explain EQU as a clearly measurable complex trait. The exact logic is less important than the architecture: intermediate capabilities can be immediately useful and later reused.
% SOURCE: Complexity definition and EQU description summarized from Lenski et al. (2003) and the user-provided slides; see https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01568

