### Rewarded stepping stones made the complex trait reachable

@(0.07,0.23) {w=0.25} ![Reward-all population](assets/reward-all-grid.png)
@(0.37,0.27) {size=24px, color=#55E6C1} REWARD ALL FUNCTIONS
@(0.37,0.39) {size=58px, color=#F4F1E8} 67 / 200
@(0.37,0.51) {size=24px, color=#9FB0C5} populations evolved EQU

{color=#38516C, thick=3} !line (0.61,0.20) (0.61,0.73)

@(0.69,0.24) {w=0.21} ![Control population](assets/control-grid.png)
@(0.69,0.64) {size=22px, color=#FF667D} REWARD EQU ALONE
@(0.69,0.72) {size=42px, color=#F4F1E8} 0 / 200

@(0.50,0.86) {size=24px, color=#FFB36A, anchor=center} The final objective was not enough.

% SPEAKER: Make the comparison quickly. Rewarding intermediate functions gave evolution a foothold; selecting only on the final target yielded no successes.
% SOURCE: Results and images from the user-provided 2026 ALife.pptx; Lenski et al., Nature 423, 139–144 (2003), https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01568

