### Evolution did not follow one privileged route

@(0.08,0.25) {size=74px, color=#55E6C1} 67
@(0.24,0.28) {size=29px, color=#F4F1E8} successful populations

...
@(0.08,0.45) {size=74px, color=#FFB36A} 59
@(0.24,0.48) {size=29px, color=#F4F1E8, w=0.63} lost another function while acquiring EQU or just before

...
@(0.08,0.66) {size=31px, color=#51C8F4, w=0.82} Every critical mutation was unique. So was every solution.

% SPEAKER: This is the part to connect to creature evolution. If many repertoires are viable, populations can approach the same functional challenge through different bodies, controllers, and tradeoffs.
% SOURCE: Results from the user-provided 2026 ALife.pptx; Lenski et al. (2003), https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01568

---
### The lineage reveals how the outcome was built

@(0.08,0.22) {w=0.40} ![Avida lineage](assets/lineage-example.png)
@(0.64,0.24) {size=28px, color=#55E6C1} Population view
@(0.64,0.32) {size=23px, color=#9FB0C5, w=0.29} shows branching, persistence, and the final dominant genotype

...
@(0.64,0.51) {size=28px, color=#FFB36A} Line-of-descent view
@(0.64,0.59) {size=23px, color=#9FB0C5, w=0.29} identifies which mutations added, repurposed, or discarded traits

@(0.64,0.78) {size=22px, color=#F4F1E8} Analyze ancestors—not only winners.

% SPEAKER: This is a methodological takeaway for the workshop. Final phenotypes hide the sequence of enabling steps and tradeoffs; lineage analysis exposes mechanism.
% SOURCE: Lineage image from the user-provided 2026 ALife.pptx.

