### Darwin anticipated reuse and multiple routes

@(0.08,0.22) {w=0.22} ![Charles Darwin](assets/darwin-portrait.png)
@(0.36,0.22) {size=29px, color=#D8FAFF, w=0.57} “Bear in mind the probability of conversion from one function to another.”
@(0.36,0.48) {size=29px, color=#55E6C1, w=0.57} “Different kinds of modification would … serve for the same general purpose.”
@(0.36,0.74) {size=20px, color=#9FB0C5} Charles Darwin, *On the Origin of Species* (1859)

% SPEAKER: Optional historical framing. New functions often emerge by repurposing existing structures; different modifications can solve the same general problem.
% SOURCE: Quotations and portrait retained from the user-provided 2026 ALife.pptx.

---
@(0.08,0.13) {size=21px, color=#FFB36A} DESIGN LEVER 2
@(0.08,0.25) {size=59px, color=#F4F1E8, w=0.78} Make resources scarce enough to create niches
@(0.08,0.58) {size=28px, color=#9FB0C5, w=0.74} When common strategies exhaust their own resources, rare alternatives become valuable.

% SPEAKER: Transition from stepping stones to ecology. The same capabilities now interact through shared, limited resource pools.

---
### Common strategies make their own opportunity disappear

@(0.07,0.24) {w=0.50} ![Limited resource supply](assets/resource-supply.png)

@(0.66,0.25) {size=27px, color=#51C8F4} A task consumes its resource
@(0.66,0.39) {size=27px, color=#FFB36A} Common tasks become less profitable
@(0.66,0.53) {size=27px, color=#55E6C1} Rare tasks gain an advantage

...
@(0.66,0.72) {size=25px, color=#F4F1E8, w=0.28} Negative frequency dependence keeps alternatives in play.

% SPEAKER: Explain the feedback, not the implementation details. A strategy's success reduces the benefit of repeating it and opens space for alternatives.
% SOURCE: Resource-supply visualization from the user-provided 2026 ALife.pptx.

---
### Scarcity made the complex trait far more likely

@(0.08,0.22) {w=0.18} ![Control](assets/control-grid.png)
@(0.41,0.22) {w=0.18} ![Reward all](assets/reward-all-grid.png)
@(0.74,0.22) {w=0.18} ![Limited resources](assets/limited-grid.png)

@(0.17,0.58) {size=22px, color=#9FB0C5, anchor=center} CONTROL
@(0.17,0.67) {size=46px, color=#FF667D, anchor=center} 0 / 200

@(0.50,0.58) {size=22px, color=#9FB0C5, anchor=center} REWARD ALL
@(0.50,0.67) {size=46px, color=#51C8F4, anchor=center} 67 / 200

@(0.83,0.58) {size=22px, color=#9FB0C5, anchor=center} LIMITED RESOURCES
@(0.83,0.67) {size=46px, color=#55E6C1, anchor=center} 167 / 200

@(0.50,0.84) {size=25px, color=#F4F1E8, anchor=center} Diversity became an engine of discovery.

% SPEAKER: Same target, same number of replicate populations. Limiting resource supply raised the number of EQU-evolving populations from 67 to 167.
% SOURCE: Results and images from the user-provided 2026 ALife.pptx.

---
### Scarcity opened many sustained evolutionary paths

@(0.08,0.22) {w=0.40} ![Resource-limited lineages](assets/resource-lineages.png)
@(0.70,0.25) {size=28px, color=#55E6C1} More coexistence
@(0.70,0.37) {size=28px, color=#51C8F4} More exploration
@(0.70,0.49) {size=28px, color=#FFB36A} More chances to recombine building blocks

@(0.70,0.68) {size=23px, color=#F4F1E8, w=0.25} The fastest route is often a portfolio of routes.

% SPEAKER: The plot shows multiple long-lived lineages. Connect this to maintaining alternative morphologies or controllers in a creature population.
% SOURCE: Lineage visualization from the user-provided 2026 ALife.pptx.

---
@(0.08,0.13) {size=21px, color=#51C8F4} DESIGN LEVER 3
@(0.08,0.25) {size=58px, color=#F4F1E8, w=0.82} Let selection change what counts as an individual
@(0.08,0.58) {size=28px, color=#9FB0C5, w=0.75} Major transitions unlock traits that no solitary organism can express.

% SPEAKER: This lever is structurally different. Instead of only changing which trait wins, evolution changes the unit that reproduces and competes.

---
### Major transitions create new design spaces

@(0.50,0.51) {w=0.72, anchor=center} ![Major transitions in individuality](assets/major-transitions-map.png)
@(0.50,0.88) {size=20px, color=#9FB0C5, anchor=center} groups · division of labor · coordination · development · ecological complexification

% SPEAKER: Walk only the main loop: independent individuals → groups → specialist groups → coordinated developmental patterns. Each transition changes what traits can evolve next.
% SOURCE: Major-transitions diagram from the user-provided 2026 ALife.pptx.

