### To evolve collective behavior, make the collective consequential

@(0.09,0.31) {size=24px, color=#55E6C1} 1 · HERITABLE GROUPS
@(0.47,0.31) {size=23px, color=#F4F1E8} offspring inherit group organization

@(0.09,0.47) {size=24px, color=#51C8F4} 2 · GROUP-LEVEL SUCCESS
@(0.47,0.47) {size=23px, color=#F4F1E8} collectives differ in survival or reproduction

@(0.09,0.63) {size=24px, color=#FFB36A} 3 · INTERNAL SPECIALIZATION
@(0.47,0.63) {size=23px, color=#F4F1E8} members can trade autonomy for division of labor

...
@(0.50,0.82) {size=29px, color=#D8FAFF, anchor=center} Change the unit of selection; change the reachable traits.

% SPEAKER: Phrase these as design conditions, not a taxonomy lecture. A group must persist, vary, and matter to reproduction before complex coordination can accumulate.

---
@(0.08,0.13) {size=21px, color=#FF667D} DESIGN LEVER 4
@(0.08,0.25) {size=58px, color=#F4F1E8, w=0.80} Add opponents that keep changing—and remember
@(0.08,0.58) {size=28px, color=#9FB0C5, w=0.75} Antagonistic coevolution turns yesterday’s solution into today’s vulnerability.

% SPEAKER: Introduce the Red Queen idea, but preview the surprising mechanism: diversity in the parasite population stores a memory of previous hosts.

---
### Parasites steal the CPU cycles hosts earn

@(0.06,0.19) {w=0.32} ![Host-parasite Avida system](assets/coevolution-system.png)

@(0.52,0.22) {size=25px, color=#51C8F4} Hosts perform logic functions to obtain resources
@(0.52,0.38) {size=25px, color=#FF667D} Parasites infect hosts that share a function
@(0.52,0.54) {size=25px, color=#FFB36A} Infection transfers 80% of host CPU cycles

...
@(0.52,0.72) {size=27px, color=#55E6C1, w=0.40} Escape requires a different phenotype—not merely a better score.

% SPEAKER: Keep this setup concrete. Matching functions create infection; successful parasites take CPU time. Hosts can escape by changing which function they use.
% SOURCE: Zaman et al. (2014), Figure 1, PLOS Biology 12(12): e1002023, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002023 (CC BY).

---
### Parasites drove host complexity higher and faster

@(0.06,0.19) {w=0.61} ![Parasites promote host complexity](assets/parasites-complexity.png)

@(0.72,0.24) {size=24px, color=#FF667D} COEVOLVING PARASITES
@(0.72,0.34) {size=48px, color=#F4F1E8} 17 / 50
@(0.72,0.44) {size=21px, color=#9FB0C5} evolved EQU

@(0.72,0.57) {size=24px, color=#51C8F4} NO PARASITES
@(0.72,0.67) {size=48px, color=#F4F1E8} 0 / 50

@(0.72,0.80) {size=19px, color=#9FB0C5} curing parasites reduced complexity

% SPEAKER: Red = coevolution; blue = no parasites; green = parasites experimentally removed halfway through. The push came from ongoing interaction.
% SOURCE: Zaman et al. (2014), Figure 2 and results, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002023 (CC BY).

---
### Parasite diversity created a memory of past hosts

@(0.06,0.21) {w=0.58} ![Adaptive landscape shaped by parasite memory](assets/parasite-memory-landscape.png)

@(0.68,0.23) {size=28px, color=#FF667D} Parasites broadened their host range
@(0.68,0.39) {size=28px, color=#FFB36A} old host strategies stayed vulnerable
@(0.68,0.55) {size=28px, color=#55E6C1} the fitness peak shifted toward novelty

...
@(0.68,0.73) {size=24px, color=#F4F1E8, w=0.27} Negative frequency dependence became directional exploration.

% SPEAKER: This is more than a two-state arms race. Diverse parasites collectively retain previously successful host targets, so hosts must explore previously unused—and typically more complex—functions.
% SOURCE: Zaman et al. (2014), Figure 4 and “Parasites Retain Memory of Previous Hosts,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002023 (CC BY).

---
### Coevolution selected for the ability to keep evolving

@(0.08,0.30) {size=36px, color=#F4F1E8, w=0.82} Hosts evolved genomes in which resistance-conferring mutations arose at high rates.

...
@(0.08,0.56) {size=38px, color=#55E6C1, w=0.82} Selection favored evolvability—not only the current solution.

@(0.08,0.80) {size=23px, color=#9FB0C5, w=0.79} A changing opponent rewards genotypes that can generate useful variation on demand.

% SPEAKER: This is the payoff beyond complexity. Coevolution changed the genotype–phenotype map so resistant phenotypes were easier to generate.
% SOURCE: Zaman et al. (2014), abstract and “Coevolution Promotes Evolvability,” https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002023

