### Four levers that make interesting complexity more likely

@(0.08,0.29) {size=24px, color=#55E6C1} 1 · STEPPING STONES
@(0.35,0.29) {size=24px, color=#F4F1E8} reward reusable capabilities; preserve many routes

@(0.08,0.43) {size=24px, color=#FFB36A} 2 · SCARCE RESOURCES
@(0.35,0.43) {size=24px, color=#F4F1E8} make common strategies create opportunities for rare ones

@(0.08,0.57) {size=24px, color=#51C8F4} 3 · NEW INDIVIDUALS
@(0.35,0.57) {size=24px, color=#F4F1E8} let heritable collectives reproduce and specialize

@(0.08,0.71) {size=24px, color=#FF667D} 4 · CHANGING OPPONENTS
@(0.35,0.71) {size=24px, color=#F4F1E8} couple success to a diverse, responsive antagonist

@(0.50,0.86) {size=21px, color=#9FB0C5, anchor=center} In every case: inspect the lineage, not only the champion.

% SPEAKER: This is the synthesis slide to linger on. Each lever changes the topology of what evolution can discover.

---
# Design the conditions,
## not the creature.

@(0.50,0.68) {size=22px, color=#55E6C1, anchor=center} Complexity emerges from accessible paths and persistent diversity.
@(0.50,0.76) {size=22px, color=#55E6C1, anchor=center} New levels of selection—and worlds that keep changing.

% SPEAKER: Resolve the opening question. We do not need to specify the interesting creature; we need to construct evolutionary conditions that make discovery cumulative and open-ended.

---
### What will your virtual creatures discover?

@(0.08,0.26) {size=29px, color=#F4F1E8} Which stepping stones can they reuse?
@(0.08,0.39) {size=29px, color=#F4F1E8} Which alternatives can coexist?
@(0.08,0.52) {size=29px, color=#F4F1E8} Can groups become individuals?
@(0.08,0.65) {size=29px, color=#F4F1E8} What keeps the world from standing still?

